  "worked_solution": "If we let $u=7-2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,\u27f9\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{3}^{7-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{5}^{3}{3}^{u}(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{3}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}){|}_{5}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}({3}^{3}-{3}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}(27-243) \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
